= On algorithms for strongly-connected components

== Tarjan's algorithm, pseudocode
The code originates in _Depth-First Search and Linear Graph Algorithms_ Tarjan
and is given in a slightly modernized form.

```
function tarjanSCC(V, E):
    i := 0
    S := []
    output := []
    index := {undefined for v in V}
    lowlink := {undefined for v in V}
    onstack := {false for v in V}
    for v in V:
        if index[v] is undefined:
            tarjanSCCstep(v)

    function tarjanSCCstep(v):
        index[v] := i
        lowlink[v] := i
        i := i + 1
        S.push(v)
        onstack(v) := true

        for w in E[v]:
            if index[w] is undefined:
                tarjanSCCstep(w)
                lowlink[v] := min(lowlink[v], lowlink[w])
            else if onstack[w] then
                lowlink[v] := min(lowlink[v], index[w])
        
        if lowlink[v] = index[v]:
            scc = []
            while index[S.top] ≥ index[v]:
                onstack[S.top] := false
                scc.push(S.top)
                S.pop()
            output.push(scc)

    return output
```

Observations:
1. This is a stateful algorithm with 6 pieces of state
2. lowlink[v] is only ever written by the call tarjanSCCstep(v). It is only ever read outside
  the local scope of tarjanSCCstep(v) just after the recursive call has been made. Thus, we can
  remove this as a global variable and just use ordinary return values to propagate it.

    ```
        function tarjanSCCstep(v):
            ...
            lowlink := i
            ...

            for w in E[v]:
                if index[w] is undefined:
                    lowlink_w := tarjanSCCstep(w)
                    lowlink := min(lowlink, lowlink_w)
                else if onstack[w] then
                    lowlink := min(lowlink, index[w])
            
            if lowlink = index[v]:
                ...

            return lowlink
    ```
3. Each node $v$ can be in one of the following states between calls to `tarjanSCCstep`:

    a. `index[v]` is undefined, `onstack[v]` is false
    b. `index[v]` is defined, `onstack[v]` is true
    c. `index[v]` is defined, `onstack[v]` is false. In this case, we will never
      read the index of v.

  We can represent this with the following ADT:

  ```
  data Status
    = Unvisited
    | Onstack(index)
    | Complete
  ```

Updating the code:

```
function tarjanSCC(V, E):
    i := 0
    S := []
    output := []
    status = {Unvisited for v in V}
    for v in V:
        if status[v] = Unvisited:
            tarjanSCCstep(v)

    function tarjanSCCstep(v):
        status[v] = Onstack(i)
        lowlink := i
        # keep old index around
        iv := i
        i := i + 1
        S.push(v)

        for w in E[v]:
            case status[w]:
                Unvisited:
                    lowlink := min(lowlink, tarjanSCCstep(w))
                Onstack(i):
                    lowlink := min(lowlink, i)
                Completed:
                    # do nothing
        
        if lowlink = iv:
            scc = []
            while let Onstack(j) = status[S.top] and j ≥ iv:
                status[S.top] := Completed
                scc.push(S.top)
                S.pop()
            output.push(scc)
        return lowlink

    return output
```

Now, rather than a for loop, we can transition to a stateful folds in the first loop
in the body of `tarjanSCCstep`

```
    function tarjanSCCstep(v):
        ...
        lowlink = foldl min iv [
            case status[w] of
                Unvisited → tarjanSCCstep(w)
                Onstack(i) → i
                Completed → ∞
            | w in E[v]]
```

This eliminates lowlink as even a piece of local state.

== Eliminating `S` and `output`
`S` mirrors the call stack, except that it might have "extra" nodes in between the call stack nodes
that have not yet been popped, as they are waiting to return to an earlier node to become part of the SCC.

Instead of having a global stack, we can pass them upwards as return values.

The same thing can be done with the `output`: we concatenate the SCCs from child calls and pass this as a result upwards.

```
function tarjanSCC(V, E):
    i := 0
    status = {Unvisited for v in V}
    S := []
    output := []
    for v in V:
        if status[v] = Unvisited:
            let (_, _, sccs) = tarjanSCCstep(v)
            output := output ++ sccs

    function tarjanSCCstep(v):
        status[v] = Onstack(i)
        let iv = i
        i := i + 1
        S_local := [v]
        output_local := []

        lowlink = foldl min iv [
            case status[w] of
                Unvisited →
                    let (j, S', output') tarjanSCCstep(w)
                    S_local := S_local ++ S'
                    output_local := output_local ++ output'
                    j
                Onstack(i) → i
                Completed → ∞
            | w in E[v]]

        if lowlink = iv:
            for w in S_local:
                status[w] := Completed
            # finish this SCC
            return (lowlink, [], S_local : output_local)
        else:
            return (lowlink, S_local, output_local)

    return output
```

This has two flaws:
1. We have reintroduced local state for S_local and output_local
2. We now pay the cost of repeated concatenation as we manipulate S and output values.

We can defer the concatenations: they also happen along the DFS tree that our algorithm visits. We can just directly represent this tree,
and have a separate way to concatenate it...

```haskell
data Tree a = Root a [Tree a]

flatten :: Tree a → [a]
flatten t = go t []
  where
    go (Root a cs) acc = a : foldr go acc cs
```

== A Haskell version, using trees

```haskell
data Status = OnStack Natural | Complete
-- use Nothing as the equivalent of Unvisited from before
type StatusMap k = Map k Status

-- Each node is labelled with 
type DFSTree k = Tree (k, [[k]])
data Progress = OnStack Natural | Complete

complete : Y k -> ([k], X k)
incomplete : Y k → X k
init : Natural → Y k
act : X k → Y k → Y k

-- `graph` is the equivalent of E from before
tarjanSCCStep :: (Ord k) => (k -> [k]) -> k -> State (Natural, Map k Progress) (SCCNode k)
tarjanSCCStep graph v = do
  i <- state (\(i, m) -> (i, (i + 1, Map.insert v (OnStack i) m)))
  ns <- for (graph v) \w ->
    gets (Map.lookup w . snd) >>= \case
      Nothing -> tarjanStep graph w
      Just (OnStack j) -> pure (SCCNode (Min j) EmptyD EmptyD)
      Just Complete -> pure (SCCNode (Min i) EmptyD EmptyD)
  let n@(SCCNode (Min d) ks kss) = foldl' (<>) (SCCNode (Min i) (ConsD v EmptyD) EmptyD) ns
  if d == i
    then do
      let ks' = toListD ks
      for_ ks' (\w -> modify (second (Map.insert w Complete)))
      pure $ SCCNode (Min d) EmptyD (ConsD ks' kss)
    else pure n

tarjanSCC :: (Ord k) => (k -> [k]) -> k -> [[k]]
tarjanSCC graph v = toListD $ (\(SCCNode _ _ kss) -> kss) $ evalState (tarjanStep graph v) (0, mempty)

tarjanSCC' :: (Ord k) => (k -> [k]) -> [k] -> [[k]]
tarjanSCC' graph vs =
  toListD $
    fold $
      evalState
        ( for vs $ \v ->
            gets (Map.lookup v . snd) >>= \case
              Nothing -> tarjanStep graph v <&> (\(SCCNode _ _ kss) -> kss)
              Just _ -> pure EmptyD
        )
        (0, mempty)
```

== Bibliographic notes
The 'path-based SCC' is very similar to Tarjan's, just that instead of
storing lowlink numbers, it has a further stack which is popped until the item
at the top has the right index. One can think of this as eagerly computing the action
of the lowlink values a separate stack.

https://home.cs.colorado.edu/~hal/Papers/DFS/pbDFShistory.html

Also, the idea of using Union-Find goes at least as far back to Ian Munro in 1971. This
case of union-find can be done in linear-time, as shown by Gabow and Tarjan in _A linear-time algorithm for a special case of disjoint set union_.